---
import Prose from "../layouts/Prose.astro";
import { REVIEWER, personUrl } from "../lib/people";
import { NMETA } from "../lib/nutrition";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";
---
<Prose title="Editorial Policy: How We Build and Check Our Pages | Gunther Heller" h1="Editorial policy" path="/editorial-policy/"
  description="How Gunther Heller builds its nutrition pages: USDA data only, no invented numbers, a registered dietitian reviewing our standards, clear corrections and advertising rules."
  lead="Every nutrition number on Gunther Heller comes from a public USDA dataset. This page explains how we turn that data into pages, who checks the work and what we will never do.">
  <h2>Our sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Nutrition facts</strong> for generic and restaurant foods: USDA FoodData Central (Foundation Foods, SR Legacy, FNDDS 2021–2023).</li>
    <li><strong>Store-brand products</strong>: the Nutrition Facts label each brand owner submits to USDA FoodData Central (Branded Foods).</li>
    <li><strong>Storage times</strong>: USDA FoodKeeper and FSIS guidance.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Every food page names the USDA record it uses and links to it, so you can check any number yourself. See the <a href="/methodology/">data methodology</a> for the details.</p>

  <h2>How pages are written</h2>
  <p>Our {NMETA.count.toLocaleString("en-US")} food pages are built by software from the USDA data: the tables, serving maths, rankings and the short sentences that describe them (“1 cup of … contains … calories”) are generated from the numbers, so text and numbers cannot disagree. Hand-written parts (food names, storage tips, our own questions and answers) never restate a number from memory.</p>
  <p>We publish a page for search engines only when people actually look for that food. Other USDA records stay available to visitors but are marked noindex. We add restaurant and store-brand items the same way: when people search for their nutrition.</p>

  <h2>Dietitian review</h2>
  <p>Our editorial standards are reviewed by <a href={personUrl(REVIEWER)}>{REVIEWER.name}, {REVIEWER.credentials}</a>, a registered dietitian nutritionist. She checks how we choose USDA records, calculate servings and % Daily Values, and phrase what the numbers mean. Pages she has reviewed individually show "Reviewed by" with the date of her review; we never show that line on a page she has not checked.</p>

  <h2>What we will never do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Invent, estimate or round a number in a way the source does not support. A nutrient USDA did not measure shows a dash, not a zero.</li>
    <li>Claim we tested, tasted or lab-analysed a food. The analyses are USDA's or the manufacturer's, and we say which.</li>
    <li>Give medical advice. Our pages are general information; talk to a doctor or registered dietitian about your own diet.</li>
    <li>Let advertisers or brands influence which foods appear, how they rank or what we say about them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Advertising</h2>
  <p>Gunther Heller is free and funded by display advertising (Google AdSense). Ads are placed by the ad network, are labelled as ads and never change our data or rankings. We do not accept payment for coverage.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If a number looks wrong, email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> with the page and your source. We check it against USDA within a few days. If we got it wrong, we fix the page and update its date. If the error is in USDA's data, we report it to USDA and note it on the page.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/contact/">contact</a> or <a href="/about/">about us</a>.</p>
</Prose>
